2 CONT Married	
2 CONT About the González de Rubalcaba surname
2 CONT
2 CONT From an article By John P. Schmal Published on LatinoLA: February 27, 2012
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rubalcaba's Come to Nueva Galicia
2 CONT
2 CONT The first known Rubalcava to arrive in the area of Nueva Galicia (Jalisco, Zacatecas and Aguascalientes) was Matheo González de Rubalcava, who was allegedly a native of Liérganes, Santander, born sometime around 1490. His wife was
2 CONC  Juana María Gonzalez, the daughter of the conquistador, Ruy Gonzalez. One of his sons was Capitán Alonso Gonzalez de Ruvalcaba, who eventually moved to Teocaltiche (located in present-day Jalisco, just south of the State of Aguas
2 CONC calientes and each of Nochistlán in Zacatecas) and is the ancestor of many Rubalcava's in that area.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that the surname González de Rubalcava implies "the González family from the place called Rubalcava." However, after a few generations in Mexico, most descendants discarded the Gonzalez but retained Rubalcava or som
2 CONC e variant of it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Teocaltiche
2 CONT
2 CONT The surname Gonzalez de Rubalcava arrived in Teocaltiche in northern Jalisco in the second half of the Sixteenth Century, when Capitán Don Alonso Gonzalez de Rubalcava and his wife, Doña Beatriz Lopez de Fuenllana, arrived in the 
2 CONC area. As stated earlier, Alonso was the son of Matheo de Rubalcava and of Juana María Gonzalez. Some sources have claimed that Alonso was born in Mexico City, but the researcher Mariano González-Leal believes that he was probably 
2 CONC born in Heras, a small town 14 kilometers from Santander and just a short distance from Liérganes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Capitán Alonso married Beatriz Lopez de Fuenllana, a native of Patzcuaro (in present-day Michoacán) and the daughter of "el conquistador," Juan López de Baeza y Santaela and of Isabel Pérez de Fuenllana. Baeza had travelled with N
2 CONC uño de Guzmán, in his path across Jalisco and Zacatecas in the early 1530s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among other things, Alonso Gonzalez de Rubalcava was a talented mason who played a major role in building the second Cathedral of Guadalajara between in the late 1560s, working at the direction of the master builder, Martin Casill
2 CONC as. Details about the life of Alonso de Rubalcava (or Robalcaba) have been discussed in the following article by Mariano González-Leal: "Alonso de Robalcaba: Estudio biográfico sobre uno de los grandes genearcas alteños," publishe
2 CONC d in the SHHAR Genealogical Journal, Volume 5 (2003), pp. 63-77.
