0 @N6000000005024488001@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT Francisco Gomez, 34, born 1576 in Coina Portugal; died in Santa Fe, NM.He married  Ana Robledo Romero 1626 in Sante Fe, NM.
1 CONT Ana Robledo Romero  She was the daughter of 106. Bartolomé Romero II and Luisa López Robledo.
1 CONT Notes for Francisco Gomez:Francisco Gomez born in Coima Portugal (5 leagues from Lisbon) son of Manuel Gomez and Ana Vicente both of whom died when he was a child. Reared by his older brother Fray Alvaro Gomez, a Franciscan, he th
1 CONC en passed onto the household of Don Alonso de Oñate at the court of Madrid. Oñate brought him to México City to join the young colony of Don Juan de Oñate.  1604 is the year he sailed to the New World.Francisco Gomez became the mo
1 CONC st outstanding military official in New Mexico during his life time occupying every office of importance including the High Sheriff of the Holy Office (Inquisition).  He was appointed Interim governor in 1641 by Governor Flores (o
1 CONC n his deathbed)  but he was not accepted by the hostile council of native New Mexicans.  His Portugese origin did not help so that even after his death he was accused of being a Jew, not only by birth, but in secret practice.  It 
1 CONC is possible that he was of Jewish extraction.
1 CONT Gomez married the daughter of Bartolomé Romero, Ana a native of San Gabriel.  They had 7 children who were known under the compound name of Gomez Robledo.  Excerpts from "Origins of New Mexico Families" by Fray Angelico Chávez.
1 CONT ______________________________________
1 CONT Sephardic Jew.
1 CONT This excerpt is from an article entitled, ALFONSO TORO'S LA FAMILIA CARVAJAL AND THE CONVERSOS OF New Mexico
1 CONT
1 CONT Frances Hernández
1 CONT The University of Texas at El Paso
1 CONT http://tell.fll.purdue.edu/RLA-archive/1993/Spanish-html/Hernandez,Frances.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Among the more urbanized, educated Sephardic descendants, most regard the discovery as an interesting part of their heritage. Others, such as Felipé Martínez, a dentist in Longmont, have decided to continue in the ancestral tradit
1 CONC ion; he has recently seen his son through his bar mitzvah. Berta Trillo, who had been called a Jew by her fellow Catholics in younger days, now sees her grandchildren brought up as Jews, guarding the five-hundred-year-old rituals 
1 CONC observed in the New World. Ramón Salas has converted to Judaism after his genealogical search through seventeen generations, as has Dennis Durán, who finds he is related to Salas (Almond 26). A contracts administrator in the Los A
1 CONC lamos laboratory, Durán found that he is descended on both maternal and paternal sides from Gómez Robledo, who came to northern New Mexico with Juan de Oñate in 1598. This early settler is famous in colonial history as one of the 
1 CONC few Judaizers whom the Inquisition chased all the way from México City into the upper Río Grande Valley; his was also an extremely rare case of acquittal, probably because he turned out to have a large personal guard. More than tw
1 CONC o hundred pages of the trial records in the annals of the Holy Office are devoted to this case.
1 CONT ____________________________________
1 CONT Title of Hidalgo
1 CONT
1 CONT A question has always existed as to whether any of the original colonists of 1598 were ever granted the title that they were promised as part of the contract for settling New Mexico. This title is namely, that of "Hijo-dalgo" or H
1 CONC idalgo. Part of the doubt as to whether this title was ever granted was that no documentation had surfaced confirming the grant of title. 
1 CONT
1 CONT José Esquibel recently uncovered a list of papers that had belonged to Francisco Gómez Robledo (ONMF: 35). This list of papers was mentioned in BONM Volume 3. Among those papers were papers that had belonged to Francisco's father,
1 CONC  Francisco Gómez (ONMF: 35 ). Among those was a "Cédula Real de un Caballero Hidalgo." This confirmed that the senior Francisco Gómez was granted this title, but just exactly how that title was worded remained unknown.
1 CONT Recently, Charles Martínez y Vigil found in the New Mexico State Archives a book entitled, History of New Mexico by Gaspar Pérez de Villagrá. This edition was translated by Gilberto Espinosa and published by the Quivira Society in
1 CONC  1933. In the Appendices are numerous papers written to or about Gaspar Pérez de Villagrá. Apparently these are all part of a collection of Villagrá's personal papers, though no mention is made of the source, these can all be assu
1 CONC med to be located in Spain.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Appendix E of this book is found the following:
1 CONT
1 CONT Title of Caballero and Hijo-dalgo in favor or Captain Gaspar de Villagra and his Descendants. 1603.
1 CONT
1 CONT Don Juan de Oñate, governor and captain general, adelantado, discoverer and pacifier of the kingdoms and provinces of New Mexico and the adjoining and neighboring regions, in the name of the king, our lord:
1 CONT
1 CONT To you, honored sir, brave and discreet, Captain Gaspar de Villagra, procurador general of the army, judge advocate [for juez asesor] of the church, of the council of war, head and agent of the royal hacienda:
1 CONT Whereas the majesty of Caesarean and royal liberality is evidenced in no better way than by fitly rewarding those who deserve its benefits, honors, and dignities, that they may on the one hand receive proper remuneration and recog
1 CONC nition for their virtues and deserts, and on the other hand that others may, with hope of such rewards, take notice and aspire with greater fervor and increasing virtue to like recompense, lending similare service; and although it
1 CONC  be true that virture is its own reward, yet it is always fittingly followed by such remuneration as its glory, honor, and excellence entitles it to. But especialy is this proper when great princes by their decree and order approv
1 CONC e and honor them.
1 CONT
1 CONT For the above reasons, considering the many and good services you have rendered to the king, our lord, and the outstanding virtures of your person, and their just deserts; the noble and exemplary habits of your life, your great ex
1 CONC perience in many affairs, your god judgment, industry, and skill, your ability in war, all of which virtues you are endowed with, as I well know, having witnessed them on many occasions, in peace as well as in war. And so, fitting
1 CONC ly to reward your efforts and labors and that you may enjoy the privileges which pertain to hijos-dalgo, and so be known, having complied with your duty, and in furtherance of what his majesty has promised to the pacifiers and con
1 CONC querors of these kingdoms, newly affirmed and renewed in a royal degree referred to the secretary, Juan Ibarra, which is as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT Don Philip, by the grace of God, king of Castile and Aragon, [etc.].
1 CONT
1 CONT Whereas the viceroy Don Luis de Velasco by virtue of a royal decree of the king, our lord, may he live in glory, entered into a contract with Don Juan de Oñate, for the discovery, pacification, and settlement of the provinces of N
1 CONC ew Mexico, which is in New Spain, and among other things he granted to him what is contained in one of the chapters of the instructions on new discoveries and settlements in the Indies, which is as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT That those who bind themselves to settle and shall have settled in thse lands and have complied with their agreement, in order to honor their persons and their descendants, and that a glorious memory may remain of them as the orig
1 CONC inal settlers, we declare them and their legitimate descendants hijos-dalgo de solar (of the lands owned by them) that in the settlements they establish and in any other part of the Indies they may be hijos-dalgo and persons of no
1 CONC ble lineage and as such may be considered and known and enjoy all the benefits, privileges, and immunities thereto pertaining and do all things that hijos-dalgo and caballeros may in the kingdoms of Castile, according to the privi
1 CONC leges, laws, and customs of Spain.
1 CONT
1 CONT With reference to the petition of Don Juan de Onate, I have been requested to grant him the grace to approve the above, notwithstanding the limitations made therein by the Count of Monterey, and I have considered it proper to appl
1 CONC y said prerogative to those who served five years in the conquest with the proviso that if any have died before serving five years in the conquest then their sons and descendants shall enjoy said prerogatives; for the present I or
1 CONC der that all those who may have gone to serve me in said conquest, pacification, and settlement, and according and in conformity with said chapter, and remained five years in said conquest, and all those who in said conquest died,
1 CONC  they and their sons and descendants shall be guaranteed all the preeminences, prerogatives, exemptions, and liberties, according to and as conceded them and set forth in said chapter, entirely and completely, failing in nothing.
1 CONT
1 CONT I order the infantes, prelates, dukes, marquises, counts, knights, priors of the Orders, prefects and sub-prefects, alcaldes of the castles and walled towns and of my council, presidents and judges, alcaldes, sheriffs of my housho
1 CONC ld and of the court, and the judges of this kingdom and the seigniories of the Indies, isles and tierra firma of the ocean, and other persons of every character or quality and condition, to guard, comply with, and execute this my 
1 CONC franchise and grace that it may have the full force of law, as though it were executed and promulgated by the courts, and that it may be announced in such parts and places as convenient.
1 CONT
1 CONT Given at San Lorenzo, July 8, 1602.
1 CONT
1 CONT I, the King
1 CONT
1 CONT I, Juan de Ibarra, secretary to the king, have done this, his command.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT By virtue of the above, I declare you, the said Captain Gaspar de Villagra, as such a pacifier and conqueror of those kingdoms, and having served the royal crown of Castile like a most noble soldier and prudent captain, for which 
1 CONC you merit the enjoyment of those liberties and privileges, exemptions and immunities by which hijos-dalgo de solar are recognized and held, fully and to all effects, in favor of yourself, your sons, descendants, and successors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Given and sealed with the seal of my arms in the villa of San Gabriel of the province of New Mexico, on October 1, 1603.
1 CONT
1 CONT Juan de Oñate
1 CONT
1 CONT By command of the governor,
1 CONT
1 CONT Juan Martinez de Montoya, secretary
1 CONT
1 CONT The above shows that the title of "Caballero Hidalgo" was in fact bestowed upon certain people. Whether it was given to every settler, or to only a few of those who were loyal to Oñate is hard to say. Only the above for Villagrá a
1 CONC nd the other in the possession of Francisco Gómez Robledo have come to light. Gerónimo Márquez was one of Oñate's loyal officers, and was also banished from New Mexico along with Villagrá. And then there was the senior Pedro Roble
1 CONC do who died along the trip in 1598, and his own son, Pedro Robledo II who died at Ácoma. All of these may have also been given this title.
